---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";

import IoModelDiagram from "../../../../assets/courses/foce/img/io-model-diagram.svg";
import BinaryByteDiagram from "../../../../assets/courses/foce/img/binary-byte-diagram.svg";

//#region CodeSnippets
const HelloWorld = `print("Hello, world!")`;
const ExampleOutput = `Hello, world!`;
const PseudocodeExample = `INPUT the person's name
SET greeting to "Hello, " joined with the name
DISPLAY greeting`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/foce/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 1.1: Computer Architecture</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 1.1</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Computer Architecture</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>How a Computer Works</p>
              <p>Input, Processing, Output, and Memory</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>The Four Basic Functions</h2>
              <p>
                Every computer, from a laptop to a microwave, performs the
                same four basic functions.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>The Four Basic Functions</h2>
              <Image src={IoModelDiagram} alt="Diagram showing Input flowing into Processing (CPU), Processing flowing into Output, and Memory connected to Processing" width={640} />
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Input</h2>
              <p>Information a computer receives from the outside world.</p>
              <ul>
                <li class="fragment">Keyboard and mouse</li>
                <li class="fragment">Microphone and camera</li>
                <li class="fragment">Sensors (temperature, motion, light)</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Output</h2>
              <p>Information a computer sends back out to the world.</p>
              <ul>
                <li class="fragment">Monitor and speakers</li>
                <li class="fragment">Printer</li>
                <li class="fragment">LEDs and motors</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Processing</h2>
              <p>
                The <b class="text-sn-theme">CPU</b> (Central Processing Unit)
                is the "brain" of the computer. It carries out instructions
                and makes decisions, one step at a time.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Memory</h2>
              <p>
                Computers need somewhere to hold data while they work, and
                somewhere to keep it long-term.
              </p>
              <ul>
                <li class="fragment">
                  <b>RAM</b> &mdash; temporary, cleared when the power turns
                  off
                </li>
                <li class="fragment">
                  <b>Storage</b> (like a hard drive or SSD) &mdash; permanent,
                  kept even after the power turns off
                </li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Binary</h2>
              <p>
                Underneath everything a computer does, it only understands two
                states: <b class="text-sn-theme">on</b> and <b class="text-sn-theme">off</b>.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                We represent those two states as <CodeSnippet
                  code="1"
                  lang={"python"}
                  theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                  inline
                /> and <CodeSnippet
                  code="0"
                  lang={"python"}
                  theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                  inline
                />. Each one is called a <b>bit</b>.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Binary</h2>
              <Image src={BinaryByteDiagram} alt="Diagram showing eight bits, 01000001, grouped together as one byte representing the letter A" width={620} />
              <p class="fragment">
                Eight bits grouped together make one <b>byte</b>.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Programming Languages</h2>
              <p>
                A <b>programming language</b> lets us write instructions in a
                form that's easier for humans to read and write, which then
                gets translated down into the binary a computer actually
                runs.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Programming Languages</h2>
              <p>There are many programming languages, each with its own rules:</p>
              <ul>
                <li class="fragment">Python</li>
                <li class="fragment">JavaScript</li>
                <li class="fragment">Java</li>
                <li class="fragment">C++</li>
              </ul>
              <p class="fragment">
                This course uses <b class="text-sn-theme">Python</b>.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Code Editors</h2>
              <p>
                A <b>code editor</b> is a specialized text editor built for
                writing and running code &mdash; it can highlight syntax,
                catch errors, and run your programs, all in one place.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Code Editors</h2>
              <p>
                There are many. Your teacher will tell you which one this
                class uses &mdash; the ideas are the same in all of them.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Identifying input, processing, output, and memory</li>
                <li>Understanding binary as a computer's "language"</li>
                <li>Writing and running your first Python program</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Input</dt>
                  <dd>Information a computer receives from the outside world.</dd>
                  <dt>Output</dt>
                  <dd>Information a computer sends back out to the world.</dd>
                  <dt>Processing (CPU)</dt>
                  <dd>The part of the computer that carries out instructions.</dd>
                  <dt>Memory</dt>
                  <dd>Where a computer holds data, either temporarily (RAM) or long-term (storage).</dd>
                  <dt>Binary</dt>
                  <dd>A system of representing information using only two states, on and off.</dd>
                  <dt>Bit</dt>
                  <dd>A single binary digit, either 0 or 1.</dd>
                  <dt>Byte</dt>
                  <dd>A group of eight bits.</dd>
                  <dt>Programming Language</dt>
                  <dd>A structured set of rules for writing instructions a computer can run.</dd>
                  <dt>Code Editor</dt>
                  <dd>A program built for writing, testing, and running code.</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <p>
            Open your coding environment and write your very first Python
            program.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: Set Up Your Environment</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Follow your teacher's setup guide to open your coding
              environment. Different setups work differently &mdash; the guide
              covers the one this class uses.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: Start Your First Program</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Create a new Python program named{" "}
              <b class="text-sn-theme">1-1-hello</b>.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <div class="border-start border-3 border-primary ps-3 my-3">
            <p class="mb-0">
              <b class="text-sn-theme">About naming.</b> Every activity tells
              you what to name your work. Depending on your setup that might be
              a project, a workspace, or a file &mdash; the name is what
              matters, so your work can be found and graded.
            </p>
          </div>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Write and Run Hello World</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Type the following line of code into your file.</li>
            <li>Run your program and check the terminal for the output.</li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={HelloWorld}
            frame="code"
            lang="python"
            title="Computer Architecture"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />


          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 4: Plan a Program &mdash; No Code Yet</h3>

          <p>
            You just ran a one-line program. Real programs are longer, and the
            hardest part is usually not the typing &mdash; it is working out{" "}
            <i>what the steps are and what order they go in</i>.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Pseudocode</b> is how programmers plan
            that out. You write the steps in plain language, in order, with no
            programming syntax at all. No quotes, no parentheses, nothing to
            get wrong. For this class, use these three words:
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li><b class="text-sn-cyan">INPUT</b> &mdash; something comes in from the user</li>
            <li><b class="text-sn-cyan">SET</b> &mdash; the computer works something out and remembers it</li>
            <li><b class="text-sn-cyan">DISPLAY</b> &mdash; something goes out to the screen</li>
          </ul>

          <p>Here is a plan for a program that greets someone by name:</p>

          <Code
            code={PseudocodeExample}
            frame="none"
            lang="txt"
            title="Pseudocode &mdash; greeting a person"
            showLineNumbers={true}
          />

          <p>
            Notice that those three words line up with three of the four basic
            functions from today: <b>Input</b>, <b>Processing</b>, and{" "}
            <b>Output</b>.
          </p>

          <h4 class="mt-4">Your Turn</h4>

          <p>
            Write the pseudocode for a program that takes{" "}
            <b>three quiz scores</b> and displays their <b>average</b>. You
            will actually build this program in Activity 1.8 &mdash; today you
            are only planning it.
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>Use only <b>INPUT</b>, <b>SET</b>, and <b>DISPLAY</b>.</li>
            <li>One step per line, in the order they must happen.</li>
            <li>
              Next to each line, label it <b>Input</b>, <b>Processing</b>, or{" "}
              <b>Output</b>.
            </li>
            <li>
              Then answer this in one sentence: <b
                >could the computer work out the average before all three
                scores have been entered?</b
              > Why or why not?
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            There is no Python to write and nothing to run for this task. If
            you catch yourself typing a parenthesis, you have drifted into
            code &mdash; go back to plain words.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>Verify your program works correctly.</p>

          <Code
            code={ExampleOutput}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            title="Example Output"
          />
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer the following questions before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>
              Which of the four basic functions (input, processing, output,
              memory) do you think is most similar to a human's brain?
              Explain your reasoning.
            </li>
            <li>
              Why do you think computers only use binary (0s and 1s) instead
              of the decimal numbers people use every day?
            </li>
            <li>
              What is the difference between a programming language and a
              code editor?
            </li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit the required files to the appropriate dropbox.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
